  "worked_solution": "First, we note that \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{log}\u2061x}{x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(10)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(10)-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(10)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)}{{x}^{2}{ln}^{2}\u2061(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(10)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0$ on $(0,\\infty )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(10)} & =0 \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061x & =1 \\\\ x & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=e$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=1,{e}^{2}$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(1) & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1)}{(1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(10)}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(10)}>0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}({e}^{2}) & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{2})}{(e)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(10)}=-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(10)}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,e)$ | $e$ | $(e,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | ${e}^{2}$ |\n| ${f}^{\u2032}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $\u2197$ | max | $\u2198$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=e,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=e$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=e.$",
